                <pb facs="0332" n="218" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg218"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00R296/4222: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Hungary</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Roosevelt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Budapest</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-07-01T11:00:00+01:00">July 1, 1931—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 1—10:05 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>19. Reference is made to the Department’s telegram No. 8, June 29, 8 p.m. The
                    Government of Hungary is concerned that the President’s proposal may have the
                    result of opening up again the extremely complex question of Eastern reparations
                    and the optants. I am informed by the Prime Minister that the Hungarian
                    Government is studying this matter before replying.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Roosevelt</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[No formal acceptance by Hungary of the President’s proposal has been found
                        in Department files, although Hungary did accept; see letter from the
                        Secretary of State to the Chairman of the Ways and Means Committee of the
                        House of Representatives, December 16, 1931, printed on <ref target="#pg240"
                            >page 240</ref>; also Great Britain, Cmd. 4052, Hungary No. 1 (1932):
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Protocol Providing for Suspension of Certain
                            Payments Due by Hungary Under the International Agreements of April 28,
                            1930.</hi>]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
